<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Zaytuna Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zitouna Mosque, Ez-Zitouna Mosque, Great Mosque of Tunis, Zaytuna Mosque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Al-Zaytuna Mosque is the great mosque of the medina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, founded in the early 8th century and largely rebuilt under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> in the 9th century. For centuries it was also one of the leading centres of learning in the Islamic world, teaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> law and the Arabic sciences, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> studied there. Its minaret was rebuilt in the 1890s.</p></div>
